WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.155
Twenty deliveries on time in both worlds. But one
leaves a home with

00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:10.310
none. The other gives every home at least three.
LiveWorld helps game creators

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.500
test the difference.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.688
A cup becomes the well, blocks become homes, and
sticks become bridges. This

00:00:17.688 --> 00:00:23.375
input is generated. Recorded Muse Spark and SAM
outputs supplied the objects and

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:29.063
shapes. Confirmed roles and image-relative positions
become a fixed-grid world, not a metric

00:00:29.063 --> 00:00:29.500
reconstruction.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.549
The creator asks for at least twenty on-time units,
at least three at

00:00:35.549 --> 00:00:41.098
every home, and at most ten waiting turns. Muse Spark
translates this into

00:00:41.098 --> 00:00:46.646
a contract. The creator confirms it. Each repair
keeps workers, demand, deadlines and

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:47.500
production locked.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.857
Moving the lower stick looks promising: waiting falls
from twenty-two to ten turns.

00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:59.713
Yet the total stays twenty of twenty-four. The
household comparison reveals the problem.

00:00:59.713 --> 00:01:05.570
Four late units move from one home to another. This
proposal fails the

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:06.921
confirmed per-home floor.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.590
Inspect a home and its delivery receipts. Each
receipt records dispatch, arrival, bridge

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:19.180
visits and waiting, with references to the
simulator's events. Select an arrival to

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.770
replay the exact turn and highlight its courier.
Across worlds, deliveries are matched

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.500
by home and order, not assumed to be the same parcel.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.480
The recorded Meta playtester tests a dispatch change
and uses a systematic simulator

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.961
search. Balanced dispatch meets every condition:
twenty units on time, waiting seven turns,

00:01:40.961 --> 00:01:46.441
and at least three on time at every home. Four units
still arrive

00:01:46.441 --> 00:01:51.500
late. Three previously fully served homes each give
up one on-time unit.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.281
Now shift the lower bridge one cell in either
direction in both worlds.

00:01:57.281 --> 00:02:02.563
The nominal balanced design passes. One direction
breaks its waiting cap; the other

00:02:02.563 --> 00:02:07.844
breaks its on-time target. These are selected
deterministic conditions, not a statistical
reliability

00:02:07.844 --> 00:02:11.500
score. No model calls are needed for the sweep.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.958
Save the project, open it again, and recompute every
trial. The contract and

00:02:17.958 --> 00:02:23.917
checkpoints travel with the world. Export household
tradeoffs, delivery receipts and sensitivity
conditions,

00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:29.875
or a replay with its inputs. Imported success claims
are discarded. Eight predeclared

00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:35.833
interpretation checks matched the expected contracts
or refusals, one attempt each. This small

00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:39.500
integration check is not a general accuracy
benchmark.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.036
LiveWorld was built by Tang Vu for indie creators.
Meta interprets the scene

00:02:46.036 --> 00:02:52.071
and design intent, and orchestrates experiments. The
simulator verifies outcomes. Three.js renders the

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:58.107
world. This is a generated-input local prototype.
Physical capture and creator usability still

00:02:58.107 --> 00:02:59.500
need broader validation.
